															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1918720&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973900" alt="Scorpion451" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1918720)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;" >Scorpion451</a></span> <span class="odate time_1418771992 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Dec 2014 23:19</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2182105">
									

<p>The part with Alexandria makes perfect sense to me: programmer culture is notorious for using inappropriately silly, casual, or crude humor in strange places for their own amusement, since no one else but other programmers will see it unless something goes horribly wrong.</p>
<p>For instance, windows has a God Mode Cheat. It gives you a convenient all-in-one settings, system maintenance, and device status panel. (Just to be clear: the panel literally has the header &quot;GodMode&quot;, like the standard video game cheat name.) There are also an astounding number of websites that the Konami Code activates easter eggs on- for example, the United States Department of Commerce's website. (not sure if it still works, but it used to let you play asteroids- think it got the programmer in some trouble at first when it went viral, but then they decided it was good PR)</p>
<p>Only makes sense that an AI would pick up some of the same habits, if just by being constantly exposed to the pattern of behavior.</p>

							</div>

								
					
